  "body": "**Plasma verification laws: what replaces the gouge and collision checks for a through-cut.**\n\nFrom [UM10720](https://tormach.com/media/asset/u/m/um10720_1300pl_1223a_web.pdf) §1.4.2, §4.12.8, §4.13 and the LinuxCNC Plasma Primer §12:\n- **Kerf crossing.** A cut path that crosses an already-cut kerf or void (THC dives into the gap, which \"can smash the torch into the material\") is warned, naming the contour and point. A rapid across a cut-out part's area is warned too (tip-up hazard).\n- **Cut order.** A part's inner contours must be cut before the outer that frees it. A violation is refused by name.\n- **Rapids.** Rapids run at the operator's G30 Z (no CAM Z). The post must emit G30 after every M205; a missing G30 is a post bug that the law catches.\n- **Kerf against the part.** The kerf-offset path must not cut into the part's material, which is the 2D gouge (part geometry versus the kerf band).\n- **The 3D stock sim** doesn't run for plasma ops. The verdict is the 2D laws above, stated as such and not silently skipped.\n\n**Done means:** each law is pinned with a fixture that trips it and one that passes; the program verdict blocks the post on a refusal (Prime Directive).\n\nPart of #621\n",
